.TH cu "" "Command"
.SH NAME
cu \- call up and converse with another system over a serial line
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBcu [\-h] [\-l \fIline\fB] [\-s \fIspeed\fB] [\fIprofile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
cu -- terminal emulator for a serial line
Usage:	cu [ -h ] [ -l line ] [ -s speed ] [ profile ]
Options:
	-h	Half-duplex (echo locally what is sent)
	-l line	Use device 'line' instead of '/dev/modem'
	-s speed	Set the serial port speed to 'speed' (default: 1200)
Reads special '~' commands as documented under 'cu Commands' below.
.HE
.B cu
turns your terminal into a terminal on a serial line, so that you can
converse with a modem or with whatever system answers on the other
end.
By default it opens
.B /dev/modem
at 1200 baud in full-duplex mode and connects your terminal to it.
.PP
If a
.I profile
is named on the command line,
.B cu
reads `~' commands from that file (see
.B "Profile Commands"
below) before opening the port, typically to set the line, speed, and
duplex before dialing.
.IP \fB\-h\fR
Run in half-duplex mode: echo locally every character sent to the
remote system.
Use this if the remote system does not echo what you type.
.IP "\fB\-l \fIline\fR"
Use
.I line
instead of the default
.BR /dev/modem .
.IP "\fB\-s \fIspeed\fR"
Set the serial port to
.I speed
baud (one of 50, 75, 110, 134, 150, 200, 300, 600, 1200, 1800, 2000,
2400, 3600, 4800, 7200, 9600, or 19200).
The default is 1200.
.SH "cu Commands"
While connected, a line beginning with a tilde (\fB~\fR) is read by
.B cu
itself instead of being sent to the remote system:
.IP \fB~#\fR
A comment; the rest of the line is ignored.
.IP \fB~~\fR
Send a single literal tilde to the remote system.
.IP \fB~!\fR
Start a shell on the local system.
.IP "\fB~<\fIfile\fR"
Redirect further local input from
.I file
instead of the terminal.
.IP "\fB~>\fIfile\fR"
Redirect what the remote system sends into
.IR file .
Write
.B >>
.I file
to append instead of overwrite, or
.B >:
.I file
to redirect silently, without also echoing to the screen.
.IP \fB~>\fR
End output redirection begun with
.BR ~> .
.IP \fB~m\fR
Search the incoming stream for a pattern.
Not implemented in this version; it prints ``Pattern matching not
supported''.
.IP "\fB~P\fIn\fR"
Pause
.I n
seconds.
.IP \fB~q\fR
Leave
.B cu
and hang up the line.
.IP \fB~Q\fR
Leave
.B cu
without hanging up the line.
.IP \fB~f\fR
Switch to full-duplex (no local echo) mode.
.IP \fB~h\fR
Switch to half-duplex (local echo) mode.
.IP "\fB~s \fIn\fR"
Set the serial port speed to
.I n
baud.
.IP "\fB~g\fIfile\fR"
Get
.I file
from the remote system, via
.BR /etc/cuxcvr .
.IP "\fB~p\fIfile\fR"
Put
.I file
to the remote system, via
.BR /etc/cuxcvr .
.IP \fB~?\fR
Print a list of the special commands.
.SH "Profile Commands"
A
.I profile
file named on the command line is read one `~' command per line, using
the same letters as the in-session commands
.BR f ,
.BR h ,
.BR l ,
and
.B s
above, to set the duplex, line, and speed before the port is opened.
A profile line beginning with
.B ~w
tells
.B cu
to stop reading the profile and open the port; the remainder of the
profile is then pushed back to be read as further `~' commands once
the connection is up.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 1.6i
\fB/dev/modem\fR	default serial line
\fB/etc/cuxcvr\fR	file-transfer helper invoked by ~g and ~p
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B sh
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B cu
prints ``Data terminal ready'' once the serial line is open, and
``Hangup'' when the line drops.
.SH NOTES
This
.B cu
is only a terminal emulator for a single serial line; it has no
dialing directory or telephone-number database, and cannot look up or
dial a remote system by name.
Pattern matching (\fB~m\fR) is not implemented in this version.
The built-in command summary printed by
.B ~?
still describes
.B ~p
as a pattern search; in this version that letter instead starts a
file transfer, as documented above.
